#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079266} :activity/id #uuid "e1c39c5e-aeaf-5263-acdb-d77006ff3b58" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048188 :topic/id #uuid "86a3f72c-9d0a-53b0-89fd-2673ea8aa6f0" :topic/math-academy-id 624} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079266 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079698 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a differentiable function. The graph of the derivative $y=f'(x)$ is given below. Can we determine the intervals on which $f(x)$ is increasing or decreasing?\n\n![](images/5b/5ba6b1945470b0ad98c1d5b6fe909e09759fb55f5ec5444c06079404c53853ad.png)\n\nTo solve this problem, we use the following theorem:\n\n*A differentiable function $f(x)$ is increasing on the interval $(a,b)$ if the derivative $f'(x)$ is positive for every value in $(a,b).$ *\n\nWe also have the following related theorem:\n\n*A differentiable function $f(x)$ is decreasing on the interval $(a,b)$ if the derivative $f'(x)$ is negative for every value in $(a,b).$ *\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $f'(x) > 0$ on the intervals\n\n$$\n(0,4), \\quad (7,10), \\quad (10,15).\n$$\n\nAlso from the graph, we see that $f'(x) < 0$ on the intervals\n\n$$\n(4,7), \\quad (15, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is increasing for $x \\in (0,4) \\cup (7,10) \\cup (10,15)$ and decreasing for $x \\in (4,7) \\cup (15, \\infty).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fa993118-a893-58bc-848b-51a9de8b0ba4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 644 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "65be8a1e-c5fa-5ebd-902a-dde66e0a4567" :step/math-academy-id 1232 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079267}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079267 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078406 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078269 :question/id #uuid "a5911e0d-32a9-5c18-a653-301a5febea0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-335"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7768eb85-b81d-5f86-b609-5fcaeebe3028" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050438}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507507}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Increasing and Decreasing Intervals Given the Graph of a Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "544f4fa6-d49b-5f00-a3a1-bac0130d0889" :step/math-academy-id 1233 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079275}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078410 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078270 :question/id #uuid "9427433b-9b7c-5c7a-8dce-ae40c340ce23" :question/math-academy-id "e-336"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c78f5b5f-45e2-543a-b4b1-ca5a558b6f86" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507470}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Graph of a Function's Derivative: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "f75fe1ea-c070-59e4-bf40-bb82b3d73033" :step/math-academy-id 1236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079275 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079699 :tutorial/content "Recall that we can use the first derivative test to identify the relative extrema of a continuous function $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=a$ if $f'(x)$ changes its sign from *positive* to *negative* around $x=a.$\n\n![](images/99/9912cc68ed61884957907c360f172fe37582827b67e0b9873795ae475dc1e86c.png)\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=a$ if $f'(x)$ changes its sign from *negative* to *positive* around $x=a.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8da20322f6797554f2c52642fd313df78ce1a7a67f54e3c524af446398ce515.png)\n\nSo, to determine the relative extrema of a function $f(x)$ by looking at a graph of $f'(x),$ we need to pay attention to the locations where $f'(x)$ changes sign." :tutorial/id #uuid "c2a3ce52-dbfc-5cef-a5bf-db4fed3ca603" :tutorial/math-academy-id 647 :tutorial/title "Relative Extrema"} :step/id #uuid "ef1674b9-a11a-59b0-b8d4-1facf6e28874" :step/math-academy-id 1238 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079278}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078414 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078271 :question/id #uuid "2cfa09af-7bf3-5acb-ad97-20fea53a2523" :question/math-academy-id "e-337"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c1bec57-410f-53cc-a3bd-f48167777932" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048478}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507510}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Relative Extrema Given the Graph of a Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "a31f8558-9f90-529c-bdfb-02f8ea14cdd1" :step/math-academy-id 1239 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079273}}] :activity/title "Interpreting the Graph of a Function's Derivative" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078650}]}
